    "result_text": "All 21 tests pass. The implementation is complete and green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `sharesHousehold` request flag was already wired into the create-owner household-duplicate bypass (Cp10). What was missing was assigning and returning a shared `householdId` (Cp11). I added that:\n\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `householdId` string to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` (returned by both `POST /api/owners` and `GET /api/owners/{id}`) carries it. MapStruct auto-maps it by name.\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `household_id` column field with getter/setter.\n- **DB schemas** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres`) \u2014 added the `household_id` column. HSQLDB is the one the test suite uses.\n- **`AssignHouseholdId.java`** (new function) \u2014 when `sharesHousehold` is true, derives a stable identifier `HH-<16 hex>` deterministically from the SHA-256 of the normalized `lastName|address` (normalized identically to the existing duplicate check). It sets it on the new owner and back-fills any existing household members so **both** owners carry the same value. When the flag isn't set, `householdId` stays null/absent.\n- **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `assignHousehold` step (under `transaction` governance) between `assignCode` and `save`.\n\nBecause the id is derived deterministically from the household identity, any two owners at the same normalized last name + address (like `b` and `c` in the test) receive the exact same, non-blank `householdId` regardless of creation order.",
